# Distillation — book-scale references

`context/instructions/distillation.md` is the contract for turning a source too
large to hold in working context at once into a structured, load-on-demand
reference in `context/` — structure over summary, sections loaded on demand, and
a merge that folds new material into an existing reference. Read it together
with `context/instructions/ingestion.md` (conversion and archiving),
`context/instructions/wiki.md` (the page model) and
`context/instructions/development.md` (the process contract). Conversion is
`sdt doc2md`; this module is the procedure that follows it. It names no
converter and no runtime.

## When this applies (no-op rule)

- Use it when a source is too large to read whole, or must become a queryable
  reference. If the source is small enough to read in one pass, do **not**
  distil — read it (`ingestion.md`). Say which case applies.
- The module describes a procedure over `doc2md` output; it never selects a
  converter, a library or a runtime.

## Choose the mode first

State the mode before extracting — it decides the budget and the output:

- **analyze-only** — map the source and propose the structure; write no
  reference yet.
- **generate** — produce the reference.
- **fold-in** — merge new material into an existing reference.

## Map before you extract

- Build a **source map** first (headings, table of contents): the outline, not
  the content. It is navigation, never a substitute for reading the evidence.
- Process **source-native sections** (chapters, sections), carrying provenance
  per segment. Never one-shot the whole book, and never summarise a section you
  did not read.
- **Partition exhaustively and validate it.** Split TOC-first (bookmarks or
  headings), not by heuristic guesswork: the partition must cover the source
  **exactly once** — refuse gaps, overlaps and duplicates — with front/back matter
  captured explicitly, and state the **numbering semantics** (source order, not
  printed page numbers). When this does not apply: a source with no usable
  structure falls back to section-native boundaries, and the refusal is reported,
  never silently bypassed.

## Extraction ledger and reconciliation

- **Keep a cumulative extraction ledger.** Alongside each segment, record a
  concise source-level summary and the concepts, claims, definitions and
  unresolved questions already extracted; carry it forward so later segments see
  prior context and repeated or evolving ideas surface. When this does not apply:
  a single-pass source needs no ledger.
- **Separate local extraction from global reconciliation.** First record
  candidates against the current segment; only after all relevant segments are
  covered, reconcile them against the whole source and the existing corpus before
  proposing creates or updates. This elaborates the dedup rule — it is not a new
  knowledge layer.
- **Locate the evidence at extraction time.** Record the source, section and
  page/line where conversion provides it, and verify before a claim enters a
  proposal that its citation resolves to the archived source and supports the
  claim. Do not invent a page number the conversion did not preserve.
- **The source thesis is conditional.** A source-level thesis and its
  load-bearing claims can orient an argumentative work, but are not mandatory for
  every source (a reference manual or dataset may have none); they never replace
  claim-level evidence.

## Structure over summary

- One file per section, loaded on demand, plus a core index.
- The core carries the table of contents, a topic index and a glossary.
- Do not dump the book, and do not mirror its chapters as prose summaries: the
  reference is a structure to navigate, not a shortened copy.

## Section contract

Each distilled section is the fold-in unit and follows one output shape:

- **Frontmatter** — `section` (its id/path), `title`, and its dependencies
  (`depends_on`/`required_by`, or free text) so the reading order is explicit.
- **A source-citation blockquote** right after the heading, pointing at the
  source locator (`refs/<file>@<sha>:<lines>` or the chapter/section/page), so
  every section is traceable without re-reading the source.
- **A per-content-type extraction checklist** — by section, deliberately walk
  definitions, claims, algorithms, equations and remarks; never let one content
  type (usually prose) crowd out the others. Equations keep their numbering
  (`\tag{}`); LaTeX fidelity is a known conversion loss point — re-check it.
- **The lossless-vs-curated stance, stated once**: the reference is lossless
  where content is load-bearing (definitions, numbered results) and curated
  where it is not (narrative); never present a curated section as complete.

## Worker contract

When the extraction is split across sub-agents, a worker is **read-only**: it
must not write corpus files, reserve addresses or edit ledgers. It returns a
typed **draft packet** — `proposals[]` (each a candidate section with its source
citation and evidence kind) plus a `partial` block (`status` / `reason` /
`completed` / `remaining`) when it could not finish. The orchestrator owns the
write, the merge and the closeout.

## Budget the depth

- The budget is a **shape**, not a set of constants: a reference section and a
  study section earn different depth, and depth is earned by containing a worked
  example, not by a bigger number.
- Calibrate the shape to the source; never copy another source's numbers.

## Cost pre-flight

- State the estimated output before generating, so the cost is a decision, not a
  surprise.
- For a very large source, probe it (grep, slice) rather than re-read it whole.
- **Tier the cost: text first, expensive renders on demand.** Read the text layer
  first; render pages or figures **per section on demand**, not eagerly for the
  whole document, because text is far cheaper than an image. State the tier
  chosen. When this does not apply: a source whose meaning is in the figures needs
  the render up front.

## Parallel extraction

For a source with eight or more chapters, extract in **dependency-tier batches**
(independent chapters first, then those that depend on them), not one giant
pass: write each batch's intermediate output under `context/tmp/`, give every
batch the same output contract (the section contract above), then verify and
fold-in in the main session. The tiering follows the source's dependency
structure, never a fixed chunk count.

## Fold-in merge

Folding new material into an existing reference is a **structured merge**, not a
regeneration:

- Classify the new material: **revisions** merge into the existing section;
  **additions** append after a numbered high-watermark, so numbering never
  collides.
- Stitch the references: a glossary entry gains its section, the topic index
  appends.
- Regenerate the core index under budget.
- Never silently drop or renumber an existing anchor. Any state write goes
  through the shipped CLI verbs — never hand-edit `status`, `updated` or a
  checklist marker.

## Provenance and boundary

- Every artifact cites its source; a distillation without provenance is a
  summary, not a reference.
- `ingestion.md` owns conversion and archiving; `wiki.md` owns the page model;
  this module owns the book-scale distillation and its fold-in.
- The destination is `context/` (the wiki corpus or a generated skill body),
  never a host skills directory.
- Research collects evidence and an analysis interprets it; distillation
  restructures a source into a reference — do not confuse the three.

## Quality gates

Before saying a distillation is done, run these **countable** checks; they reuse
the verify-step vocabulary (**passed** / **expected** / **inferred**) and
`sdt context lint`, never a new mechanism:

- **Completeness audit** — count sections (and theorems/algorithms/figures)
  against the source, not from memory; a missing required section **refuses**
  completion — never ship a partial as whole.
- **Cross-ref integrity** — every `depends_on` has its reciprocal `required_by`
  (and vice versa); a dangling reference is fixed or explicitly listed.
- **Stale-index rule** — generate the core index **last**, scanning the files on
  disk, never from memory; then run `sdt context reindex` and `sdt context lint`.
- **Never drop errors silently** — on a failure, save the partial output and list
  exactly what was dropped and why; a silent omission is a defect.
- **Pre-approval checklist** — record each gate as passed/expected/inferred; an
  `inferred` (not run) gate is named, never implied to have passed.

## Boundary

- Conversion and archiving defer to `context/instructions/ingestion.md`.
- The wiki page model defers to `context/instructions/wiki.md`.
- Tool choice, install and any code defer to
  `context/instructions/development.md` (library-first, simplicity, surgical
  changes).
- This module names no converter and no runtime.
